Z||000000||Flash Box||05-12-2000||02:55 PM||||Hello Everyone. <BR>First off you have a really awsome site here seems to be very friendly.<BR>I just got a 1984 SE Fiero it is black with tan interior. <BR>I am new to the Fiero scene so any information would be greatly appreciated.<BR>My plans are to make a show car so any suggestions on what would make it more eye catching please feel free to suggest.<P><BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||05-12-2000||04:16 PM||||Show car: Make it clean.  I mean everything.  Black cars are a pain, but look fantastic when clean and polished. McGuires or other companies have multiple components for cleaning car surfaces. Wash, clean (cleans the oxides off), polish, wax, Final inspection.  A random orbital buffer is real helpful.  Lots of cotten rags. They look like wash cloths (Pep Boys will have bags of them $4/ a dozen). If I do 5 of the 7 step process, it takes about 4 hours and two dozen cotton rags.<P>Clean the wheel wells.  They get beat, but once the dirt is gone you can freshen the color with black paint.  Look for the can with the Fiero on the label.  Clean the wheels.  Usually the wheels are beat, lots of elbow grease to clean up oxidized aluminum wheels or $95-$135 each to get them refinished.<P>Clean under the hood.  Take out the spare tire and clean out any leaves, dirt etc.  Wipe down all the black painted surfaces.<P>Clean the engine compartment.  Gunk solvent or something like that can get old grease deposits etc. Follow directions on the can.<P>Interiors: shampoo carpets.  The seats are each held in place by 4 nuts.  Then when you remove seats you can really get all the carpet. You can even do the seats if they are dirty.<P>A little Armor All or similiar material on the dash, center console.  (Don't overdo the stuff).  Use this on weather stripping.  Mothers "Back to Black" on black plastic moldings, front cowling, windshield rubber gasket.<P>Enough work to keep the car shining that you won't have any time to get into trouble.  All the work that you put into it, you won't want to go out and beat it up, knowing all the work you'll need to do to get it back into shape.<P>Or you can take the car to a detail shop and they can do an acceptable job for a couple of hundred $$.  But once done, its easy to keep up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||Homicidal||05-12-2000||04:20 PM||||Agreed!  I keep my near immaculate all the time!||||reg||1
